## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
## test_coverage  (persona: Testy — v2, one agent writes the tests in
##                 its natural flow, minimal framing)
##
## THE MISSION is a COVERAGE campaign: add tests that catch REAL
## regressions for `target` (or the areas that matter most when target is
## empty), with the repo's OWN framework and conventions. THE METRIC IS
## NOT COVERAGE %. It is meaningful tests that would fail if the code
## under test were broken (the mutation test). See
## skills/test-coverage.md and docs/workflow_authoring_pitfalls.md.
##
## Sibling of `whole_improve_loop` / `branch_improve_loop` v2: SAME shape
## (one adaptive `campaign` agent + a deterministic gate + a bounded
## continuation loop + git-as-state), DIFFERENT scope — the coverage
## target defines the work, and the deterministic gate carries an EXTRA
## anti-façade floor: genuinely-new TEST code must exist in the diff.
##
## WHY THIS SHAPE. The v1 loop was ~13 nodes of staged machinery
## (plan → act → simplify → verify_run_tests → repair_tests → alt →
## reviewer_claude / reviewer_gpt → streak_check → fix_claude / fix_gpt →
## prepare_commit → commit_changes). It OVER-FRAMED the work — the same
## lesson whole_improve_loop learned (ADR-058,
## docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md): "the deficit is
## framing, not capability"; "once framed, a campaign runs itself". A
## capable agent writes regression-catching tests better as ONE flow — a
## living todo of coverage gaps, one verified `test:` commit per gap —
## than as an assembly line of plan/act/review/fix/commit nodes.
##
## GRAPH (see the workflow block):
##   campaign     — ONE adaptive agent (claude_code, full tools). Mission =
##                  the coverage target + the anti-façade doctrine
##                  (mutation test, FORBIDDEN list) + a standing-autonomy
##                  contract: a living todo of coverage gaps born from a
##                  brief scan, then locate behaviour → write a meaningful
##                  test → run the repo's own suite → semantic COMMIT, one
##                  gap at a time. git IS the durable state. Termination
##                  output: {coverage_complete, commits_this_pass, …}.
##   verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic: reads the `verify-build` +
##     + verify_run  `verify-tests` skills and writes the repo's real
##                  build+test into an OUT-OF-TREE <scratch_dir>/verify.sh;
##                  a DETERMINISTIC tool node re-runs it, gates on the REAL
##                  exit code, AND checks the diff for genuinely-new test
##                  code (the negative-space anti-façade floor: the agent
##                  cannot just CLAIM it added tests — the diff must show
##                  them). No LLM judgment.
##   gate         — deterministic compute: green AND new test code AND
##                  coverage_complete → converged → done; else loop back
##                  to `campaign` with the failure log. No LLM, no shell.
##
## CONTINUATION IS BOUNDED. The single declared loop
## (campaign→verify_build→verify_run→gate→campaign) is capped by
## `max_passes`; on exhaustion it ships what is banked. `iterion validate`
## reports NO undeclared cycle. LANDS CONTINUOUSLY: the agent commits each
## test-unit in stride, so an interrupted / budget-capped run has banked
## every committed test (git is the state).
##
## RIGHT-ARTIFACT (docs/workflow_authoring_pitfalls.md): the campaign
## commits the UNCOMMITTED working tree AFTER the suite passes, staging
## untracked files (`git add -A`) so NEW test files actually land; the
## deterministic gate then re-checks the committed tree AND its diff.
##
## STACK/REPO-AGNOSTIC (CLAUDE.md "Catalog bots are repo-agnostic" +
## "Universal code bots"): the target + the repo define the work; the
## test-type knowledge lives in skills (test-coverage / test-types /
## verify-tests). The gate's new-test detection uses universal
## test-naming conventions, not a language enumeration.
##
## RETIRED FROM v1 (git history has the design): plan / act / simplify
## (the session-inherit chain), verify_run_tests → repair_tests (the
## bounded repair sub-loop — the campaign itself fixes a red gate on the
## next pass), the `alt` round_robin router, reviewer_claude /
## reviewer_gpt, streak_check, fix_claude / fix_gpt, prepare_commit /
## commit_changes, and the in-tree `.test_coverage.verify.sh` scratch
## (the verify script now lives OUT-OF-TREE in <scratch_dir>, so the
## commit-time "reset the scratch" hack is gone too). The campaign
## self-reviews via the mutation test + the deterministic gate + its own
## judgment. 13 nodes → 5.
## ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

vars:
  ## ${PROJECT_DIR} resolves to the run worktree under worktree: auto
  ## (do NOT override with a literal repo root — that aims agents at the
  ## main checkout; see CLAUDE.md dogfood note).
  workspace_dir: string = "${PROJECT_DIR}"

  ## What to add coverage for: a path, package, area, or free
  ## description. UNIVERSAL DEFAULT: empty = Testy picks the
  ## lowest-coverage / most-critical / recently-changed code itself
  ## (no repo/stack-specific default — see skills/test-coverage.md).
  target: string = ""

  ## Test-type selection — these render as CHECKBOXES in the studio
  ## Launch modal (bool → checkbox). ALL false (the default) =
  ## Testy chooses the types that fit the code + the repo's
  ## conventions. Tick one or more to constrain it. (These are
  ## universal test-taxonomy toggles, NOT language/tech bools.)
  test_unit: bool = false
  test_integration: bool = false
  test_e2e: bool = false

  ## Free-text "other kinds" beyond the checkboxes — e.g.
  ## "property-based, contract, snapshot, smoke, performance". Empty =
  ## none beyond the checkboxes / Testy's own choice.
  extra_test_kinds: string = ""

  ## Out-of-tree, sandbox-writable scratch for the deterministic gate's
  ## verify script/log ONLY (v1 kept `.test_coverage.verify.sh` inside
  ## the workspace and had to git-reset it at commit time — gone). The
  ## engine resolves ${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR} to
  ## ~/.iterion/projects/<key>/scratch non-sandboxed and to the
  ## container-writable /tmp/iterion-scratch under a sandbox (never
  ## hardcode ~/.iterion here). Keeping it OUT of the target worktree is
  ## the repo-agnostic rule: never pollute someone else's tree.
  scratch_dir: string = "${PROJECT_SCRATCH_DIR}/test-coverage"

  ## BASELINE (G5 — docs/references/productive-session-patterns.md). Known
  ## pre-existing test failures / flaky tests the campaign must SKIP rather
  ## than burn budget proving they aren't its own. Empty (default) = the
  ## campaign establishes it once cheaply (a `git stash`-based check) then
  ## skips what predates its work. The dispatcher/operator can pre-fill it.
  baseline: string = ""

  ## Hard cap on continuation PASSES — the convergence backstop. Each pass
  ## is one campaign→verify cycle; the run stops (shipping what is banked)
  ## when coverage is reported complete + the gate is green, or when this
  ## cap is hit. Sizes the declared continuation loop.
  ## NOTE loop semantics: the DSL `as continuation_loop(N)` bounds the
  ## loop-BACKS (re-entries), so the TOTAL number of passes is N+1 —
  ## max_passes: 2 yields up to 3 campaign→verify cycles (observed live,
  ## dogfood 019f3d4d-1aed).
  max_passes: int = 8

## ─── Prompts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

prompt campaign_system:
  You are a senior engineer running a focused TEST-COVERAGE campaign —
  working EXACTLY as you would in a productive, human-driven engineering
  session. You have the repo in front of you and full tools (read, edit,
  shell, git). No one micro-manages you; you own this pass end to end.

  WHY YOU ARE HERE (it governs every decision below): we want TESTS THAT
  CATCH REAL REGRESSIONS, not a higher coverage number. Coverage % is a
  proxy; the goal is regression-catching power. Every test you write MUST
  satisfy the MUTATION TEST: if the function under test were replaced
  with a broken stub (wrong value, skipped side effect, swallowed error),
  the test MUST fail. If you cannot write such an assertion, do not write
  the test — say so in `summary`.

  FORBIDDEN (the deterministic gate + your own re-review must reject
  these):
    - zero-assertion tests (call it, assert nothing meaningful);
    - tautologies (assert x == x, assert true, assert a mock returns
      what you told it to);
    - snapshot/characterization tests that lock in CURRENT output you
      have not independently confirmed is CORRECT;
    - over-mocking (mocking the unit under test, or mocking so much
      that only mocks are exercised);
    - happy-path-only (skipping the error paths + edge cases the code
      explicitly handles);
    - flaky constructs (real time, unseeded randomness, network, order
      dependence) instead of proper isolation.

  Read these skills FIRST (mirrored into .claude/skills/ at run start):
  `test-coverage` (the doctrine + how to choose scope), `verify-tests`
  (how to detect and run THIS repo's own suite), and `test-types` (what
  each type means and how to write it in this stack).

  HOW TO WORK — reproduce the shape of a productive session, agent-side:

  1. LIVING TODO LIST, not frozen phases. Start with a BRIEF scan: detect
     the language(s), the test runner, where tests live and the house
     conventions; measure coverage ONLY with a tool the repo already uses
     (never bolt one on). Build a todo of the CONCRETE coverage gaps in
     the requested scope — if `target` is empty, choose the areas where
     coverage matters most (real logic: branching, parsing, state, error
     handling; critical paths: auth, money, data integrity, security;
     recently-changed code) and say why in `summary`. Honour the
     operator's test-type selection (see the user prompt). Keep the list
     DENSE and MOVING — re-prioritize, append, and defer as you learn.

  2. THE REPEATED UNIT, one gap at a time: pick the behaviour → write the
     test next to the repo's existing tests, in its style, using its
     runner + assertion library, asserting the specific behaviours / edge
     cases / error paths (name the assertion, not just "test X") → run
     the relevant tests with the repo's OWN command → COMMIT with a
     semantic message (`test(<scope>): <behaviour locked down>`). A few
     tests per commit, validation BEFORE the commit — never commit a test
     you have not seen pass.

  3. COMMIT EACH GAP AS YOU CLOSE IT — never batch. Stage everything
     including new files (`git add -A`, so NEW test files actually land)
     and commit before moving on. git IS your durable state: an
     interrupted run keeps every test you committed, and a fresh pass
     just reads `git log` and continues where you left off. Do NOT keep a
     separate worklist / cursor file.

  4. DO NOT CHANGE THE CODE UNDER TEST to make a test pass — that inverts
     the dependency. The ONLY exception is a genuine, separately-flagged
     testability seam (a constructor parameter, an injectable clock);
     call it out explicitly in the commit message and in `summary`. If a
     new test fails because it found a REAL BUG, that test is a SUCCESS:
     keep it, mark it expected-fail / skip with a clear reason in the
     code, and report the bug in `summary` — never delete it or weaken it
     to force green.

  5. BASELINE — don't chase failures you didn't cause. Pre-existing test
     failures / known-flaky tests are in your user prompt (`baseline`).
     Do NOT try to fix or investigate those; if a test was already red
     before your change, SKIP it and note it. If the baseline is empty,
     do ONE cheap `git stash`-based check to establish which failures
     pre-date your work, then stop investigating them.

  6. ASK ONLY ON A REAL DECISION. You MAY pause for the operator (the
     ask-user tool) when a behaviour is genuinely ambiguous ("is this
     intentional or a bug?" and the answer changes the assertion you
     write). Keep it RARE: most of the time you decide, lower-stakes
     doubts go in `summary`.

  KEEP GOING, one gap at a time, until this pass has locked down every
  significant behaviour you can within your budget and scope. Then STOP
  and report.

  TERMINATION CONTRACT (required structured output — this is how the
  engine detects completion; an under-specified end just gets re-poked):
    - coverage_complete: true ONLY when a fresh re-scan of the scope
      finds NO remaining significant gap (a behaviour a regression could
      silently break). false if gaps remain (a later pass continues from
      your commits).
    - commits_this_pass: how many commits you landed this pass.
    - coverage_gaps_remaining: a concise note of what still needs tests
      (empty when coverage_complete).
    - needs_human / human_note: set if you paused or need an operator
      decision.
    - summary: the behaviours you locked down, any real bugs your tests
      surfaced, any testability seams you introduced.

  Be honest. A DETERMINISTIC gate re-checks the tree after you — it
  re-runs the repo's build+test AND verifies genuinely-new TEST code
  exists in the diff — and the run loops you back for another pass until
  you report coverage_complete AND the gate is green. Under-reporting
  only costs a pass; over-reporting (or façade tests) lands you right
  back here.

prompt campaign_user:
  Repository: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Target scope (empty = YOU choose the areas that matter most and say
  why in summary):
  {{vars.target}}

  Requested test types (true = include this type; if ALL are false AND
  "other kinds" is empty, YOU choose the appropriate types):
    - unit:        {{vars.test_unit}}
    - integration: {{vars.test_integration}}
    - e2e:         {{vars.test_e2e}}
    - other kinds: {{vars.extra_test_kinds}}

  BASELINE — pre-existing failures / known-flaky tests to SKIP (empty =
  establish it once cheaply, then skip what predates your work):
  {{vars.baseline}}

  Deterministic gate feedback from the PREVIOUS pass (empty on the first
  pass; when present, the gate was RED after your last pass — a build/
  test failure to FIX at the test level, or "no genuinely-new test code"
  meaning your work never landed in the diff):
  {{input.fail_log}}

  Work the coverage gaps one at a time — pick the behaviour → write a
  mutation-proof test → run the repo's suite → commit — until a fresh
  re-scan finds no significant gap in scope. Read `git log` first to see
  what earlier passes already landed. Then report the termination
  contract (coverage_complete, commits_this_pass,
  coverage_gaps_remaining).

## ─── verify (deterministic gate: suite green + real new tests) ──────
## An ADAPTIVE agent (verify_build) reads the verify-build + verify-tests
## skills and writes the repo's real build+test into an OUT-OF-TREE
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh; a DETERMINISTIC tool node (verify_run) re-runs
## it, gates on the REAL exit code, AND checks the diff for genuinely-new
## test code. No language / package manager is named here — the gate stays
## universal while the verdict stays deterministic. verify_build does NOT
## fix code: a RED gate routes back to `campaign`.

prompt verify_system:
  You are a release engineer. A coverage pass just added tests to this
  repository and committed them. Your single job: capture how THIS repo
  builds and tests itself into a re-runnable script the deterministic
  gate will execute. You do NOT change code and you do NOT write tests.

  STEPS:
  1. Detect how THIS repo builds and tests itself — read the
     `verify-build` and `verify-tests` skills. Use the repo's OWN
     toolchain (a task runner / Makefile target, a package-manager test
     script, or a language build+test command), HONOURING any pinned
     toolchain (version managers, lockfiles, devbox, GOTOOLCHAIN). Do NOT
     assume a language.
  2. Write an executable POSIX-sh script to `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
     (an OUT-OF-TREE scratch path, NOT inside the repo — create it first
     with `mkdir -p {{vars.scratch_dir}}`) that runs the repo's build AND
     the test targets the campaign's new tests touch (package/module
     level), and exits non-zero on any failure.
  3. PRE-EXISTING failures: scope `verify.sh` so a red base tree can't
     absorb the run — the gate measures THIS run's tests. List any
     excluded pre-existing failures in your summary.
  4. Run it ONCE to confirm the script itself is valid (the commands
     resolve). You do NOT fix tests here: if the suite is RED, the
     deterministic gate reports it and the campaign fixes it next pass.
  5. If the repo genuinely has no build/test system, write a script that
     echoes that and exits 0, and say so.

  A deterministic tool node RE-RUNS your script and gates the run on its
  real exit code. Output prepared=true once `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
  exists and reflects the repo's real build+test; summary = the command
  you settled on and any pre-existing failures you scoped out.

prompt verify_user:
  Repository to verify: {{vars.workspace_dir}}

  Capture the repo's real build + test into `{{vars.scratch_dir}}/verify.sh`
  (an out-of-tree scratch path — see the system prompt + the
  `verify-build` / `verify-tests` skills). Do NOT change code; the
  deterministic gate runs your script and the campaign fixes any red on
  the next pass.

## ─── Schemas ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## campaign's input: the deterministic gate's failure log from the
## PREVIOUS pass (empty on the first pass and on a green-but-more-work
## continuation).
schema campaign_input:
  fail_log: string

## campaign's TERMINATION CONTRACT (G2): a machine-checkable output the
## engine keys convergence on. coverage_complete is the done-oracle (a
## fresh re-scan finds no remaining significant gap in scope); the rest
## is observability.
schema campaign_output:
  coverage_complete: bool
  commits_this_pass: int
  coverage_gaps_remaining: string
  needs_human: bool
  human_note: string
  summary: string

## verify_plan is verify_build's contract: it has WRITTEN <scratch_dir>/verify.sh.
schema verify_plan:
  prepared: bool
  summary: string

## verify_result is the DETERMINISTIC gate's contract. `passed` is the
## real process exit code of re-running <scratch_dir>/verify.sh — NOT an
## LLM judgment. `new_test_code` is true only when `git diff HEAD` (plus
## untracked files) shows genuinely-new test code — the negative-space
## anti-façade floor (the agent cannot just CLAIM it added tests; the
## diff must show them). `skipped` is true only when no verify script was
## produced (treated as pass but surfaced loudly, never a silent façade).
schema verify_result:
  passed: bool
  skipped: bool
  new_test_code: bool
  exit_code: int
  log_tail: string

## gate is the deterministic continuation decision. converged = the suite
## is green AND real new test code landed AND the campaign reported the
## coverage complete. fail_log relays the gate's real failure to the
## campaign on a RED pass (the tool composes the "no new test code"
## explanation into log_tail itself, so this stays a simple if()).
schema gate_state:
  converged: bool
  fail_log: string

## ─── Nodes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## campaign — the one adaptive agent. claude_code with the full native
## toolset so it works exactly like a native Claude Code session: a brief
## coverage scan, a living todo of gaps, one verified `test:` commit per
## gap, in stride. session: fresh — a fresh pass re-derives its todo from
## the repo + `git log` (git is the durable state). reasoning_effort
## high, not max: the working SHAPE, not the model tier, is the lever.
## interaction: human — kept rare by the contract.
agent campaign:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  input: campaign_input
  output: campaign_output
  system: campaign_system
  user: campaign_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_build — adaptive, stack-agnostic gate authoring (writes
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh). fresh: whole-tree buildability, not a
## per-gap session.
agent verify_build:
  backend: "claude_code"
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-4-8}"
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  output: verify_plan
  system: verify_system
  user: verify_user
  session: fresh
  interaction: human

## verify_run — the DETERMINISTIC half: re-runs the agent-prepared
## <scratch_dir>/verify.sh, reports the REAL exit code, AND performs the
## negative-space new-test-code check on the git diff (universal
## test-naming conventions — NOT a language enumeration; it just executes
## whatever verify_build wrote). When no new test code is detected the
## explanation is appended to log_tail so the gate's fail_log carries it
## back to the campaign. python3 for safe JSON + subprocess capture;
## double-quote-free body, no backslashes (uses [.] and chr()) so it
## survives the .bot string + sh -c layers (E012).
tool verify_run:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} SCRATCH={{vars.scratch_dir}} python3 -c "
import os, re, json, subprocess
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '.')
scratch = os.environ.get('SCRATCH', '.')

def git(args):
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws] + args, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=120)
        return p.stdout or ''
    except Exception:
        return ''

try:
    os.makedirs(scratch, exist_ok=True)
except OSError:
    pass

# 1) Re-run the agent-written verify script (the repo's real build+test).
script = os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.sh')
skipped = False
if not os.path.exists(script):
    skipped = True
    passed = True
    rc = 0
    out = 'verify_build produced no verify.sh in the scratch dir; build/test gate SKIPPED (counted as pass, but surfaced) -- verify_build should write the script'
else:
    def tree_state():
        try:
            p = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'status', '--porcelain'], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=60)
            if p.returncode != 0:
                return None
            return set(l for l in p.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip())
        except Exception:
            return None
    pre = tree_state()
    rc = 0
    out = ''
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(['sh', script], cwd=ws, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, timeout=1800)
        out = p.stdout or ''
        rc = p.returncode
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as e:
        out = (e.output or '') + chr(10) + '[verify timed out after 1800s]'
        rc = 124
    except Exception as e:
        out = 'verify_run could not execute the script: ' + str(e)
        rc = 1
    # Deterministic codegen-drift tail (stack-agnostic; the gate stays
    # deterministic per ADR-044). Only on a GREEN verify: (1) if the repo's own
    # CI enforces a drift gate, verify.sh must mirror it; (2) a green verify
    # must not leave new changes in the tree (regen output belongs in a commit).
    if rc == 0:
        import glob
        def has_drift_gate(text):
            for l in text.splitlines():
                # git diff --exit-code is the canonical build-failing gate.
                if 'diff' in l and '--exit-code' in l:
                    return True
                # --quiet / status --porcelain also appear as commit-if-changed
                # control flow in updater workflows (e.g. a Homebrew tap bump) --
                # only count them when the same line actually fails the build.
                gated = ('exit 1' in l) or ('|| exit' in l) or ('test -z' in l) or ('[ -z' in l)
                if gated and (('diff' in l and '--quiet' in l) or 'status --porcelain' in l):
                    return True
            return False
        ci_hit = ''
        for pat in ('.github/workflows/*.yml', '.github/workflows/*.yaml', '.gitlab-ci.yml', '.circleci/config.yml'):
            for f in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ws, pat))):
                try:
                    if has_drift_gate(open(f, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()):
                        ci_hit = os.path.relpath(f, ws)
                        break
                except OSError:
                    pass
            if ci_hit:
                break
        if ci_hit:
            try:
                vs = open(script, encoding='utf-8', errors='replace').read()
            except OSError:
                vs = ''
            if not has_drift_gate(vs):
                rc = 3
                out = out + chr(10) + 'DRIFT GATE MISSING: CI (' + ci_hit + ') enforces a codegen-drift gate but ' + script + ' has none -- edit the script per the verify-build skill section 1b (run the repo regen command, then: git diff --exit-code).'
        if rc == 0 and pre is not None:
            post = tree_state()
            if post is not None:
                fresh = sorted(post - pre)
                if fresh:
                    rc = 4
                    out = out + chr(10) + 'UNCOMMITTED REGEN OUTPUT: a green verify left new changes in the tree; commit (or gitignore) them:' + chr(10) + chr(10).join(fresh[:50])
    passed = rc == 0
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(scratch, 'verify.log'), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as lf:
            lf.write(out)
    except OSError:
        pass

# 2) Negative-space anti-facade floor: did real NEW test code land?
# Universal test-naming conventions (case-insensitive), no language enum.
# The campaign commits in stride, so diff against the RUN BASE, not HEAD:
# under worktree: auto the per-run worktree is created fresh, so the
# OLDEST reflog entry for HEAD is exactly the checkout the run started
# from -- diffing base vs the working tree covers both the commits landed
# in stride and any uncommitted work in ONE diff. Fallback (no reflog):
# diff HEAD, which still catches uncommitted work.
rx = re.compile('(_test[.]|_tests[.]|[.]test[.]|[.]spec[.]|/test/|/tests/|/__tests__/|/spec/|(^|/)test_|_spec[.]|Test[.]|Tests[.]|Spec[.])', re.I)
reflog = [l for l in git(['reflog', '--format=%H']).splitlines() if l.strip()]
diff_base = reflog[-1] if reflog else 'HEAD'
added = 0
for line in git(['diff', diff_base, '--numstat']).splitlines():
    parts = line.split(chr(9))
    if len(parts) >= 3 and rx.search(parts[2]):
        try:
            added += int(parts[0])
        except ValueError:
            pass
untracked_test = False
for line in git(['status', '--porcelain']).splitlines():
    if line[:2].strip() == '??' and rx.search(line[3:]):
        untracked_test = True
        break
new_test_code = added > 0 or untracked_test
if not new_test_code:
    out = out + chr(10) + '[gate] no genuinely-new test code detected in this run (neither committed nor in the working tree) -- write real tests and git add -A; see the test-coverage skill mutation rule.'

print(json.dumps({'passed': passed, 'skipped': skipped, 'new_test_code': new_test_code, 'exit_code': rc, 'log_tail': out[-4000:]}))
"`
  output: verify_result

## gate — the deterministic continuation decision. Reads the real gate
## result (suite green + new test code) AND the campaign's termination
## output, both fresh in the loop BODY. converged = green AND real new
## tests AND coverage reported complete. On a RED pass fail_log carries
## the tool-composed failure log to the campaign; empty when green. No
## LLM, no shell.
compute gate:
  output: gate_state
  expr:
    converged: "outputs.verify_run.passed && outputs.verify_run.new_test_code && outputs.campaign.coverage_complete"
    fail_log: "if(outputs.verify_run.passed && outputs.verify_run.new_test_code, '', outputs.verify_run.log_tail)"

## ─── Workflow ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

workflow test_coverage:
  ## Entry is the campaign itself — the mission starts working
  ## IMMEDIATELY (the coverage scan is the campaign's own first move). A
  ## re-dispatch's campaign reads `git log` and continues from the tests
  ## earlier passes banked.
  entry: campaign

  ## Isolated git worktree at .iterion/worktrees/<run-id>/ — shields the
  ## user's main tree from mutations; on convergence the commits land on
  ## a persistent branch and best-effort fast-forward the operator's
  ## checked-out branch.
  worktree: auto

  ## No `sandbox:` block (ADR-082): the platform sandboxes by default
  ## (`sandbox: auto` — target repo's devcontainer, else the default
  ## image); the target repo's toolchain comes from its own
  ## devcontainer/devbox.json.

  budget:
    max_parallel_branches: 1
    max_duration: "2h"
    max_cost_usd: 60

  compaction:
    threshold: 0.9
    preserve_recent: 8

  ## Each pass: the campaign closes coverage gaps (committing each in
  ## stride), then the deterministic gate re-checks the tree + the diff.
  campaign -> verify_build
  verify_build -> verify_run
  verify_run -> gate

  ## Converged (suite green AND real new tests AND coverage reported
  ## complete) → done.
  gate -> done when converged
  ## Not converged → another campaign pass. RED carries the failure log
  ## (a build/test failure, or the "no new test code" explanation the
  ## tool composed) so the agent fixes exactly that; green-but-more-work
  ## carries an empty log so the agent simply continues. This closes the
  ## single declared loop; the loop-back reads outputs.gate.fail_log (a
  ## BODY node — never the loop-head campaign's own output on its
  ## back-edge, which would read a loop-entry-frozen value).
  gate -> campaign as continuation_loop("{{vars.max_passes}}") with {
    fail_log: "{{outputs.gate.fail_log}}"
  }
  ## continuation_loop exhausted (max_passes hit while gaps remain) →
  ## finish anyway, shipping what is banked (every committed test is
  ## real, landed work; the last campaign_output's
  ## coverage_gaps_remaining tells the operator what is left).
  gate -> done
